To find the Jacobian matrix of the vector-valued function \(\vec{F}(x,y,z) = \begin{pmatrix} f(x,y,z)\\ g(x,y,z) \\ h(x,y,z)\end{pmatrix}\), we need to compute the partial derivatives of each component function \(f\), \(g\), and \(h\) with respect to each variable \(x\), \(y\), and \(z\). The Jacobian matrix is given by:

\[
J_{\vec{F}}(x,y,z) = \begin{pmatrix}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial f}{\partial z} \\
\frac{\partial g}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial g}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial g}{\partial z} \\
\frac{\partial h}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial h}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial h}{\partial z}
\end{pmatrix}
\]
